5:## Mapping the neuroanatomical heterogeneity of OCD using a framework integrating normative model and non-negative matrix factorization
31:Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) is a spectrum disorder with high interindividual heterogeneity. We propose a comprehensible framework integrating normative model and non-negative matrix factorization (NMF) to quantitatively estimate the neuroanatomical heterogeneity of OCD from a dimensional perspective. T1-weighted magnetic resonance images of 98 first-episode untreated patients with OCDandmatchedhealthycontrols(HCs, n = 130) were acquired.We derived individualized differences in gray matter morphometry using normative model and parsed them into latent disease factors using NMF. Four robust disease factors were identified. Each patient expressed multiple factors and exhibited a unique factor composition.Factor compositions of patients were significantly correlated with severity of symptom,age of onset,illness duration,and exhibited sex differences,capturing sources of clinical heterogeneity.In addition, the group-level morphological differences obtained with two-sample t test could be quantitatively derived from the identified disease factors, reconciling the group-level and subject-level findings in neuroimaging studies. Finally, we uncovered two distinct subtypes with opposite morphological differences compared with HCs from factor compositions. Our findings suggest that morphological differences of individuals with OCD are the unique combination of distinct neuroanatomical patterns. The proposed framework quantitatively estimating neuroanatomical heterogeneity paves the way for precision medicine in OCD.
33:Key words : gray matter volume; individualized analysis; subtypes of OCD; OCD.
45:studies reveal that OCD symptom dimensions are linked to distinct patterns of comorbidities (Hasler et al. 2005), genetic polymorphisms(Hasler et al.2006),impairments in distinct neural circuits (Mataix-Cols et al. 2004), and differential responses to pharmacological and nonpharmacological treatments (MataixCols et al. 1999; Mataix-Cols et al. 2002). Although the dimensional approaches based on symptomatology show potential benefits in identifying neural markers relate to illness course and treatment response, several controversies to their implementation remain (Gilbert et al. 2009). (i) The correct number of factors and the appropriate factor structure for OCD are still controversial (Summerfeldt et al. 1999; Bloch et al. 2008). (ii) Similar clinical features or symptom patterns can be caused by different underlying mechanisms, limiting these features' usefulness to differentiate etiologically distinct patients (Hasler 2010). (iii) The dimensional approach based on symptomatology has a vague diagnostic threshold in terms of how to handle sub-threshold symptoms, and some patients may not clearly belong to any subtype (Okada et al. 2015). (iv) More seriously, subtypes based on symptomatology may not be stable over time (Lahey et al. 2005). Recently, researches turn to performing dimensional analyses on objective neuroanatomical data such as structural magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) data to resolve the heterogeneity in neuropsychiatric disorders including depression, autism, epilepsy, and Alzheimer's disease (Zhang et al. 2016; Chen et al. 2022; Lee et al. 2022; Shan et al. 2022).
47:A number of approaches are put forward to resolve the morphological heterogeneity of gray matter in neuropsychiatric disorders using objective MRI data. Normative model can infer individualized neuroimaging differences of a patient by measuring its deviates from the healthy distribution (Marquand et al. 2016). Using this method, researchers successfully quantify structural brain heterogeneity at subject level in mental disorders (Wolfers et al. 2018; Zabihi et al. 2019; Wolfers and Beckmann 2020). For example, using the normative model, Wolfers et al. find that the individualized gray matter morphological differences in schizophrenia and bipolar disorder would be concealed by traditional case-control designs, providing a step toward a systematic mapping of the heterogeneity of mental disorders (Wolfers et al. 2018). The similar conclusions are found in attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (Wolfers and Beckmann 2020) and autism spectrum disorder (Zabihi et al. 2019). Individualized neuroimaging differences further help to identify more homogeneous subtypes with distinct (even opposite) neuroanatomical differences profiles (Liu et al. 2021; Shan et al. 2022) and clinical manifestations, reconciling the conflicting findings of neuroimaging studies and facilitating personalized clinical decision-making in mental disorders (Han, Xu, Guo, Fang, Wei, et al. 2022a, 2022b, 2022e; Han, Zheng, et al. 2022; Shan et al. 2022). However, these pioneer studies do not provide effective methods to deal with the high-dimensional results derived from the normative model (Wolfers et al. 2018; Wolfers and Beckmann 2020). Additionally, the quantitative association between subjectlevel differences derived from the normative model and grouplevel differences are not well elucidated. In other words, previous studies cannot explain how subject-level differences contribute to the observed group-level differences, leading to a lack of interpretability of subject-level differences.
49:To quantitatively estimate the heterogeneity of OCD, we proposed a novel framework integrating the normative model and non-negative matrix factorization (NMF) from a dimensional perspective. This framework obtained subject-level differences in gray matter morphometry measured with voxel-based morphometry analysis (VBM) using the normative model
51:(Ashburner and Friston 2000) and parsed them into latent disease factors using non-negative matrix factorization (NMF). As a promising method widely used in MRI studies, NMF decomposed high-dimensional data into a factor matrix (latent disease factors) and a weight matrix (factor compositions) (Anderson et al. 2014; Shan et al. 2022). Thus, individualized morphological differences were represented as a linear combination of the identified disease factors. The framework assigned each patient a unique factor composition, thus retaining interindividual variability. We posited that each patient with OCD expressed multiple latent disease factors rather than one single factor. In this study, we would show that factor compositions were associated with clinical characteristics capturing sources of clinical heterogeneity. We further showed that our framework could significantly infer factor compositions of unseen patients. What' more, we demonstrated that group-level morphological differences could be quantitatively derived from the identified disease factors, reconciling the group-level and individualized findings in neuroimaging studies. Finally, we uncovered two putative OCD subtypes with opposite morphological differences compared with HCs from factor compositions.
53:## Methods
59:Ninety-eight first-episode and drug-naive patients with OCD were recruited from outpatient services of the Department of Psychiatry, the First Affiliated Hospital of Zhengzhou University. The diagnosis was made by two experienced psychiatrists independently according to the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Fifth Edition (DSM-V) for OCD. The symptom severity was evaluated by the Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale (Y-BOCS) (Goodman et al. 1989). None of the patients had nervous system disease, brain trauma, comorbid, other mental/psychotic disorders, first-degree relatives with a history of mental disorders, or neurological diseases. One hundred and thirty healthy controls (HCs) were recruited from the community through poster advertisement. In addition, all participants were Han Chinese, righthanded,andhadtomeetthefollowing exclusion criteria: (i) taking drugs such as anesthetics and analgesics in the past 1 month; (ii) substance abuse; (iii) a history of a brain tumor, trauma, surgery, or other organic body diseases; (iv) suffering from cardiovascular diseases, diabetes or hypertension; (v) contraindications for MRI scanning; and (vi) other structural brain abnormalities.
61:## Data acquisition
63:T1-weights MRI scans were acquired using 3-Tsela GE Discovery MR750 scanner (General Electric, Fairfield Connecticut, USA) at the First Affiliated Hospital of Zhengzhou University. The 3D T1-weights scans were acquired as follows: repetition time (TR) = 8164 ms, echo time (TE) = 3.18 ms, flip angle = 7 ◦ , resolution matrix = 256 × 256, voxel size = 1 × 1 × 1 mm 3 , flip angle = 7 ◦ , slices = 188, thickness = 1.0 mm.
67:The main analysis process had six steps in this study. First, subject-level morphological (gray matter volume, GMV) differences were derived using the normative model. Second, individualized GMV differences were parsed into latent disease factors using non-negative matrix factorization (NMF). As NMF needed a
69:Fig. 1. A) Illusion of the dimensional approach where a patient is expressed as a combination of potential disease factors. B) The flow chart of our framework. The individualized GMV differences are derived from the normative model and then parsed into latent disease factors with the optimal number of latent disease factors ( K ). Then, we associate factor compositions (weights) with clinical characteristics. Robust analysis is done to examine reproducibility of our results. We investigate the association between individualized differences and group-level differences using a regression model. Finally, clustering analysis is performed to reveal OCD subtypes.
79:We obtained GMV for each subject using the Computational Anatomy Toolbox (CAT12, http://dbm.neuro.uni-jena.de/cat12/). Main steps included: (i) segmenting images into gray matter, white matter and cerebrospinal fluid; (ii) normalizing segmented images into Montreal Neurological Institute space and resampled them to 1.5 × 1.5 × 1.5 mm; (iii) nonlinear modulation; and (iv)
81:the gray matter images were smoothed with an 6-mm full width at half maximum Gaussian kernel (Ashburner 2009; Han et al. 2021). We also calculated the total intracranial volume (TIV) for the following analysis. The Image Quality Rating (IQR) combing image noise contrast ratio, inhomogeneity contrast ratio and root mean square was recorded to assess images quality (Brown et al. 2019; Han, Xu, Guo, Fang, Wei, et al. 2022a, 2022b, 2022e).
85:Combing the normative model and NMF, we proposed a framework that allowed one patient to express multiple latent factors rather than assigned it to a single category. The main assumption of our framework was that each patient with OCD was a combination of multiple latent disease factors. There were two main steps in modeling disease factors.
87:First, we obtained personalized morphological differences for each patient with OCD using the normative model by evaluating
93:its deviates from the normal distribution. Following the previous studies (Marquand et al. 2016; Wolfers et al. 2018), we trained a Gaussian process regression model to predict voxel-wise GMV from age and sex in HCs and then applied the trained model to infer GMV of patients with OCD. For each voxel, the Z -score was obtained by quantifying the deviation between the predicted GMV andthetrueonethennormalizedbytheuncertaintyof prediction. The positive Z -scores suggested increased GMVs than expected in patients than HCs and vice versa. We divided voxel-wise morphological differences into increased and decreased parts according to Z -scores. For decreased parts, the Z -scores were multiplied by -1. The following step was performed in increased and decreased parts separately.
95:Then, we identified the potential disease factors from individualized morphological differences using non-negative matrix factorization (NMF). As a data-driven multivariate method, NMF produced a sparse, parts-based representation of the data under non-negativity constraints (Lee and Seung 1999). Compared with independent component analysis (ICA) and principal component analysis (PCA), the results of NMF were more explanatory (Sotiras et al. 2017). In addition, NMF yielded a soft (probabilistic) parcellation of voxel-wise aberrance and was proved to accurately identify subject-specific functional networks (Li et al. 2017) and reveal individualized coordinated patterns of cortical development during adolescence (Sotiras et al. 2017 be expressed as the following formula:
99:In this study, D represented Z -scores matrix (subject × voxel), H represented the disease factors (the number of latent factors K × voxel), W represented the corresponding weights (factor compositions) of subjects (subject number × K ), and /epsilon1 was the residuals. The number of latent disease factors ( K ) needed to be pre-defined.
