   111	## Examining the extendibility of our framework on unseen patients
   112	
   113	To examine the extendibility of our framework, we investigated whether the identified disease factors could be used to infer factor compositions of unseen patients using leave-one-out cross validation (LOOCV). For each validation, disease factors were obtained from training dataset and then were used to infer factor compositions of the patient in test dataset using a linear regression model. The coefficients of the regression model were treated as its factor compositions. The Pearson's correlations between the predicted factor compositions and the true ones (obtained using all patients) were calculated. The significance was evaluated using permutation testing (1,000 times).
   114	
   115	## Association with the results of group-level analysis
   116	
   117	Then, we investigated how the individualized differences contributed to the observed group-level differences. The group-level differences were obtained with two sample t test where age, sex, and TIV were included as covariates. This procedure was done using SPM12 (http://www.fil.ion.ucl.ac.uk/spm). We constructed a linear regression model to predict the group-level differences (unthresholded T map) from the identified 4 disease factors. Here, we supposed that the group-level differences were representative of an 'average' patient and the coefficients of the regression model were assumed as its factor compositions.
   118	
   119	## Clustering analysis
   120	
   121	Finally, to further verify whether the identified disease factors could capture clinical heterogeneity in OCD,we performed affinity propagation clustering to uncover putative subtypes of OCD from factor compositions (Frey and Dueck 2007).
