217:these characteristics were consistently identified as sources of heterogeneity leading to inconsistent findings in OCD. For example, studies frequently reported that there were sex differences in genetic architecture, epidemiological, and clinical characteristics such as age of onset, the course of disease, and symptom dimension (Khramtsova et al. 2019; Mathes et al. 2019). Female patients tended to show a later onset and were accompanied by symptoms related to contamination while male patients were often accompanied by inappropriate thoughts (Mathes et al. 2019). We reproduced sex differences in terms of onset in our data (female patients vs. male patients, P =0.041, t =2.077, Cohen' d =0.451). Accordingly, neuroimaging studies recognized sex differences in neuroimaging phenotype in OCD (Boedhoe et al. 2017; Weeland et al. 2022). In accordance with these findings, we found that sex differences were reflected in differences weight in positive factor 2 and increased weights of negative factor 2. Age of onset was another confounder hampering unified findings in OCD. Patients with OCD were usually further divided into early-onset and late-onset subtypes according to the illness onset, and these two subtypes exhibited differences in genetic load, symptom profiles, comorbidities, treatment responses, and neuroimaging differences (Taylor 2011; Anholt et al. 2014; Kim et al. 2020). In neuroimaging studies, researchers found that early-onset patients tended to exhibited larger volumes in distributed brain regions than lateonset patients, possibly reflecting an increased environmental sensitivity, delayed dendritic pruning, or compensatory changes (Nickson et al. 2016; Blank and Meyer 2022). Correspondingly, in our results, the weights of positive factor 2 showed significant negative correlation with age of onset, suggesting the earlier illness, the larger gray matter volume in OCD than healthy population. In addition, weights of the identified disease factors were significantly correlated with the severity of symptom and illness duration, pronouncing the identified disease factors were relevant to neuronal substrates and potential neuroplastic changes as the disease prolonged.
